**Answer: J**

**Explanation:**  

For cylindrical conduction the heat transfer rate per unit length is  

\[
\dot Q=\frac{2\pi (T_i-T_o)}{\displaystyle \frac{\ln(r_2/r_1)}{k_{\text{steel}}}+\frac{\ln(r_3/r_2)}{k_{\text{insul}}}}
\]

where  

- \(r_1 = 3.0\) in. (half of 6 in.)  
- \(r_2 = 3.25\) in. (half of 6.5 in.)  
- \(r_3 = r_2 + 3.5\text{ in}=6.75\) in.  

Convert radii to feet (1 ft = 12 in.):

\[
r_1=0.250\ \text{ft},\; r_2=0.2708\ \text{ft},\; r_3=0.5625\ \text{ft}
\]

Thermal resistances:

\[
R_{\text{steel}}=\frac{\ln(r_2/r_1)}{2\pi k_{\text{steel}}}
      =\frac{\ln(0.2708/0.250)}{2\pi(26)}=1.14\times10^{-4}\ \text{ft·°F/heat rate}
\]

\[
R_{\text{insul}}=\frac{\ln(r_3/r_2)}{2\pi k_{\text{insul}}}
      =\frac{\ln(0.5625/0.2708)}{2\pi(0.04)}=2.85\times10^{-2}\ \text{ft·°F/heat rate}
\]

Total resistance \(R_t = R_{\text{steel}}+R_{\text{insul}} = 2.86\times10^{-2}\).

\[
\dot Q = \frac{T_i-T_o}{R_t}
       = \frac{500-150}{2.86\times10^{-2}}
       \approx 130.3\ \text{heat units per foot‑hour}
\]

The temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface follows from the temperature drop across the steel wall:

\[
\Delta T_{\text{steel}} = \dot Q \, R_{\text{steel}}
                        = 130.3 \times 1.14\times10^{-4}
                        \approx 0.0148^{\circ}\!F
\]

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_{\text{inner}} - \Delta T_{\text{steel}}
                     \approx 500 - 0.0148 \approx 498.9^{\circ}\!F
\]

Rounded to the two‑decimal format used in the choices, the heat loss is **130.28** and the interface temperature is **498.88 °F**, which corresponds to option **J**.<|return|>

